fold / same feature set / same target, so the three are comparable.\n# TODO: shap.summary_plot() for each, side by side (or in one figure)\n# TODO: compare each model's top-10 SHAP features against Week 6's permutation-importance\n#       top-10 for the SAME target -- do all three agree with permutation more than MDI did\n#       in Week 6 (recall: MDI vs permutation only overlapped 4-6/10)?\n","metadata":{},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"id":"5ae63822-652d-4d18-ada4-9d5a6b8be12d","cell_type":"markdown","source":"### SHAP dependence plot — same feature, three models, one shape check","metadata":{}},{"id":"110d4b2d-01f4-40e7-a1b9-5b95c581379b","cell_type":"code","source":"# TODO: pick Week 6's top permutation-importance feature (rsi_4h or ema_separation_4h)\n# TODO: shap.dependence_plot() for that feature, once per model (RF/XGBoost/LightGBM)\n# TODO: does the shape roughly match Week 6's PDP curve for the same feature, across all\n#       three models? A sanity check, not an expectation of identical curves.\n","metadata":{},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"id":"23c75853-ebea-4e04-bca3-55baa790c29e","cell_type":"markdown","source":"### SHAP waterfall plot — one good signal, one bad signal, your current best model","metadata":{}},{"id":"122c53df-61eb-44d6-8bd7-94e46d12ad52","cell_type":"code","source":"# TODO: pick one row where class_target_1 == 1, prediction confident and correct\n# TODO: pick one row where class_target_1 == 0, prediction confident and correct\n# TODO: shap.waterfall_plot() for each -- which features pushed the prediction each way?\n","metadata":{},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"id":"aeb7df65-8400-4c59-8cab-297e7d3c08d8","cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Deliverable — three-way SHAP interpretation report (fill in)","metadata":{}},{"id":"3f4b8226-ab3c-4a54-a4f9-60172ead7e11","cell_type":"markdown","source":"*(your notes here: agreement level between the three models' SHAP rankings; agreement with Week 6's permutation importance; any feature one model leans on that the other two don't, and your best guess why)*","metadata":{}},{"id":"1bd596d5-b4eb-4bdd-b336-49c5384ca481","cell_type":"markdown","source":"---","metadata":{}},{"id":"6a4d4726-c496-4de7-9084-4cc447c9807f","cell_type":"markdown","source":"## PART D — Model Stacking Theory + Implementation (Wednesday)","metadata":{}},{"id":"23372baa-413c-494a-9566-f10a84d3e45f","cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Concept:** a stacking ensemble trains a meta-model on the **out-of-fold predictions** of several level-0 models, rather than on the raw features directly. The idea is that each level-0 model (LogReg, RF, XGBoost, LightGBM) makes different kinds of mistakes, and a meta-model can learn when to trust which one — this only helps if the level-0 models are meaningfully different from each other, not just differently-tuned versions of the same idea.\n\n**Walk-forward passthrough is the part that's easy to get wrong here:** the meta-model's training rows must be *out-of-fold* predictions from the level-0 models (predictions on data those models didn't see), computed walk-forward, in the same style as `cv_no_scaling()`/`cv_regression()` — not in-sample predictions, which would hand the meta-model a leaked, over-optimistic signal to learn from.","metadata":{}},{"id":"dc411fe3-5c2e-4f6f-88a1-bb2b6f6f93ce","cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Level-0 models: LogReg, RF, XGBoost, LightGBM","metadata":{}},{"id":"4ff0b5ad-464f-4cbb-b894-810d0156b1c1","cell_type":"code","source":"# TODO: load Week 3/4's tuned LogReg, Week 6's tuned RF, Week 7's tuned XGBoost,\n#       and this week's tuned LightGBM -- one saved model per target/direction, reused\n#       here rather than retrained from scratch.\nlevel0_models = {}\n","metadata":{},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"id":"f98bdfc8-89c3-4893-9b9a-124db037e473","cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Level-1 meta-model: LogisticRegression (simple, interpretable)","metadata":{}},{"id":"68177284-0f64-4c67-a